rig SV-003): fixed (newThread sets title = cwd leaf default).\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/team-impl-log.md:101:  - SV-001 reload reconstruction: accepted-risk — reload now re-resolves from the durable LHC registry (cwd-most-recent), NOT module memory; exact-prior-thread reconstruction deferred (design forbids a PI-session->thread map and a durable resolved-id store). Approved by impl-lead under Lee's standing authorization.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/team-impl-log.md:102:  - SV-002 --resume operator selection: accepted-risk — PI v0.79.2 exposes no interactive input/selection surface (verified wiring research); picker logic is implemented + tested; production presents candidates via ctx.ui.notify and auto-selects most-recent. Approved by impl-lead. UPSTREAM REQUEST: PI extension input/selection API needed for real --resume picker + exact-thread reload.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/team-impl-log.md:108:- Acceptance note: verifier final = block on SV-001/SV-002 (PI-platform-constrained ACs). Per ls-impl, accepting a blocked finding as risk is permitted when concrete tech-arch evidence supports the interpretation AND the user accepts; the tech-arch explicitly states PI-API gaps \"constrain what an epic can promise\" and Lee gave standing authorization to rule such deviations autonomously. A-8 LHC registry work (cwd column, partial-id resolve, cwd-filtered listThreads, title) landed in packages/lhc and passes lhc verify.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/team-impl-log.md:113:- Verifier Evidence: artifacts/02-.../008-verify.json (initial revise, 3 bugs, 2 proven by repro), 011/012/013-verify.json (fresh re-verifies tracking fixes). Final residual SV-001 = unsolvable identity-less edge (see analysis above).\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/team-impl-log.md:114:- Story Gate: pnpm --filter pi-lhc verify — PASS (impl-lead run: 14 test files / 71 tests). ALSO pnpm --filter lhc verify — PASS (41 files / 389 passed, 9 skipped) — inspect/health change (SV-002 fix) caused no lhc regression.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/team-impl-log.md:119:  - SV-002/SV-003 (later rounds: unmappable-hook-input gap; per-part omission keys): fixed.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/team-impl-log.md:120:  - SV-001 residual (fresh-attach id-less + position-less + same-content event can collide): accepted-risk (documented limitation). Real PI message_end always carries a stable entry id (research line 142; production keys off it as Tier-1), so this edge is unreachable on the real hook path; it is information-theoretically unsolvable for a truly identity-less event. Hardening note below.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/team-impl-log.md:31:- 02-inference-seam-and-model-assignment\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/team-impl-log.md:57:### 02-inference-seam-and-model-assignment\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/team-impl-log.md:59:- Implementor Evidence: artifacts/02-inference-seam-and-model-assignment/005-implementor.json (ready-for-verification; 003 was a blocked first attempt from the session-limit interruption, replayed cleanly)\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/team-impl-log.md:61:  - artifacts/02-inference-seam-and-model-assignment/006-verify.json (pass, zero findings, codex/gpt-5.5)\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/team-impl-log.md:153:- Story 2: createSdk provider XOR inference; ModelCall contract; 7-kind assignment validation; routing tests assert actual ModelCall input.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/post-epic-4-work.md:62:        the configured model assignment (provider + model) for each derivation kind,\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/post-epic-4-work.md:70:Per-kind injection moves knowledge to the wrong side of the boundary. The host would hold seven near-identical functions differing only in model choice — which is config, not code — and would have to know LHC's derivation catalog, making an eighth derivation kind a breaking change to every host's wiring. With one injected function, the seven-ness stays inside LHC: prompts are product knowledge, model assignments are LHC config, and the host's wiring never changes when derivations evolve.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/post-epic-4-work.md:76:- The model-assignment config and its schema (which provider and model each derivation kind uses).\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/post-epic-4-work.md:77:- Validation: all seven assignments must resolve at init/first-drain; an assignment naming a provider that isn't logged in fails loudly for that kind — derivations land `failed` with a readable reason visible in `inspect.health`, never silent skips, never weird downstream flashes. (Misconfigured/not-logged-in providers are a problem in every design; the requirement is that it's *visible*, because the way it fails is otherwise hard for the user to see.)\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/post-epic-4-work.md:79:- **No model opinions.** LHC ships placeholder defaults that fail validation loudly rather than guessing — the seven model assignments are a conscious setup step, because a silent wrong default is exactly the hard-to-see failure mode.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/post-epic-4-work.md:84:- The model-assignment *values*, supplied at `createSdk` like view profiles and visibility budgets are today.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/post-epic-4-work.md:111:- **No runtime provider-health routing, cost ceilings, or policy logic** in the model-assignment config. That's control-surface-era (Jarvis) territory; the config stays dumb.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/post-epic-4-work.md:118:- Add the inference/derivation feature: real providers, the seven prompts, the injected model-call function, model-assignment config, validation behavior. The real-inference capstone is the integration-readiness gate — M3/M4's deferred quality halves transfer to it.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/post-epic-4-work.md:128:- PI extension wiring: the small model-call function (PI auth storage + model registry + `complete`, per-call routing); model-assignment values in config; assignments naming not-logged-in providers visible at extension load, not just first drain.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/post-epic-4-work.md:216:- **Exact model-assignment config file location/format** (LHC-schema'd file the extension points at vs. embedded in PI's extension settings). Settled in principle: the schema is LHC's, the values are host-supplied, and it's a deliberate isolation seam.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:43:| 5 | Startup validation and assignment config | Inference Host |\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:68:- Model-assignment config (the seven derivation kinds) and startup validation with visible, actionable reporting.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:76:- Status-bar / footer UX beyond actionable startup-validation reporting — Feature 3 / Epic 3.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:79:- Derivation prompt and model dial-in (quality tuning) — the paired M0 dial-in period; this epic ships default assignments and proves the routing, not the tuning.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:213:At session start, before any derivation runs, the extension validates that all seven derivation assignments point at lanes PI can reach. Unreachable lanes are reported up front, naming the kind, the (provider, model), and the fix. A validation failure does not stop capture; the affected derivations fail, classified and queryable. Assignments come from config; a user overrides any kind's provider, model, or prompt without touching code.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:215:**AC-5.1:** At session start the extension validates all seven model assignments against PI's registry before first derivation use.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:217:**AC-5.2:** An unreachable lane — not logged in, unknown model, unknown provider — is reported before first use, naming the derivation kind, the (provider, model), and the corrective action. The report reaches the user in interactive and headless modes alike (guarded on UI availability, never assuming a TUI).\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:221:**AC-5.4:** Model assignments load from the extension's config. Each of the seven derivation kinds resolves to a (provider, model, prompt), where the prompt names a registered prompt. The epic ships with default assignments so derivations function; assignment quality is a dial-in concern, not a build gate.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:223:**AC-5.5:** A user override — a different provider, model, or prompt for any kind — takes effect on the next session start with no code change. An incomplete or unknown assignment fails loudly at initialization with an actionable error, never a silent skip or a placeholder default that masks the misconfiguration.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:369:### Model assignments\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:373:assignments: Record<FormKind, ModelAssignment>   // all seven, validated complete at init\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:390:- **Headless parity.** Every user-facing behavior in this epic (startup-validation reporting) works in PI's `rpc`, `json`, and `print` modes, guarded on UI availability — never assuming a TUI. (Headless conformance as a delivered guarantee is Epic 4; the constraint is honored here so nothing built now blocks it.)\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:391:- **Fail loud on misconfiguration.** Missing, unknown, or placeholder assignments fail at initialization with an actionable error, not a silent skip.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:468:**Delivers:** Seven-assignment validation against PI's registry at session start, unreachable-lane reporting (interactive and headless), config load with defaults, user override on next start, fail-loud on misconfiguration.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:521:| AC-5.3 | TC-5.3 | 6 |\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:554:- **TC-5.1** — All seven assignments validate against the registry at session start before first use.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:555:- **TC-5.2** — An unreachable lane reports kind + (provider, model) + fix; the report appears in a headless mode, not only the TUI.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:556:- **TC-5.3** — A validation failure leaves capture running; the affected lane's derivations fail classified and queryable.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:559:- **TC-5.6** — An incomplete or unknown assignment fails loudly at initialization with an actionable error; no placeholder default masks it.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/test-plan.md:39:### `test/lifecycle/thread-resolution.test.ts`\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/test-plan.md:93:| TC-4.2 | Two kinds → different (provider,model) both route in one session | two assignments, two fakes | each call routes by its keys; both succeed |\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/test-plan.md:104:### `test/inference/startup-validation.test.ts`\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/test-plan.md:108:| TC-5.1 | All seven assignments validated against registry before first use | seven assignments, registry | validation runs at session start, before any derivation |\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/test-plan.md:109:| TC-5.2 | Unreachable lane reported with kind+pair+fix; appears headless | two assignments: (a) `modelRegistry.find(provider, model)` fails; (b) model exists but `hasConfiguredAuth(model)`/`getAvailable()` says auth is absent; headless mode (`hasUI:false`) | report names kind, (provider,model), reason, and distinct fix (`fix assignment` vs `run login/configure auth`); emitted via headless channel and stored in `SessionState.health`, not only TUI |\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/test-plan.md:110:| TC-5.3 | Validation failure leaves capture running | an unreachable lane | capture continues; affected lane's derivations fail classified + queryable |\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/test-plan.md:112:### `test/inference/assignments.test.ts`\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/test-plan.md:118:| TC-5.6 | Incomplete/unknown assignment fails loud at init | missing kind / unknown prompt / placeholder | construction throws actionable error; no placeholder masks it |\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/test-plan.md:149:- **Skeleton:** `instance.ts`, `thread-resolution.ts`, `picker.ts` as structured-result stubs.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/test-plan.md:184:- **Skeleton:** `assignments.ts`, `startup-validation.ts`.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/test-plan.md:17:| `test/view-boundary.test.ts` (amended) + `test/view-boundary-turn-end.test.ts` (new) | default | TC-5.1, TC-5.2, TC-5.3 |\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/test-plan.md:26:- `recordingCall(responses: Record<FormKind, string>)` → `{ call: ModelCall, log: ModelCallInput[] }`. Resolves each call with the kind's canned text (kind inferred from the assignment the test built — in practice keyed by `model` string, which tests assign uniquely per kind). Every input pushed to `log`.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/test-plan.md:30:- `validAssignments(overrides?)` → a complete seven-kind assignment map for construction tests, each kind assigned a distinct fake provider/model string.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/test-plan.md:36:**`test/fixtures/seam-conformance.ts`** (Chunk 0) — the parameterized contract assertions (DD-13): `assertModelCallContract(call)` (shape of success/failure results on a probe call) and `assertRoutingThroughSdk(call, assignments)` (TC-1.2's routing assertions extracted so TC-4.3 runs them against the real host unchanged).\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/test-plan.md:46:- unknown kind key in assignments → `TypeError` naming it\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/test-plan.md:49:- empty `provider`/`model` string in one assignment → `TypeError`\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/test-plan.md:64:- each form's `metadata.provenance` equals its assignment `{ provider, model, prompt }`; deterministic-provider forms carry none\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/test-plan.md:119:### TC-5.3 — Epic 03 contract regression under the new trigger (AC-5.5)\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/test-plan.md:175:| Boundary decision and status sum diverge | shared-query invariant + TC-5.3's status leg after each advance |\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:88:**Inference wiring (already specced as Epic 05's host side):** the ~15-line `ModelCall` — `ctx.modelRegistry.find(provider, model)` → missing ⇒ `{ ok: false, kind: \"auth\"|\"invalid_request\" }` → `complete(model, { messages })` → map errors to `ModelCallFailureKind`. Assignments (the seven `kind → { provider, model, prompt }`) come from the extension's config section; `createSdk` throws on incomplete/unknown assignments, and the extension surfaces that `TypeError` at load as a visible, actionable startup error — exactly the startup-validation requirement from the notes doc.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:95:4. **Inference host duties** — `ModelCall` + assignments config + startup validation surfacing.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:191:**Epic A — Connector core (intake + lifecycle + inference host):** ~5–6 stories. SDK construction/teardown on session lifecycle, thread mapping + resume/reload/fork handling, message/turn event mapper (converter productionized), `ModelCall` + assignments config + startup validation, ordering/idempotency proof against recorded corpora.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/02-event-capture-and-turn-derivation.md:29:- Derivation routing and assignment validation, owned by Stories 5 and 6.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/epic.md:19:**Mental model:** LHC owns what to ask (seven prompts, one per derivation kind) and which model to ask it of (per-kind assignment in config). The host owns how to reach models (one injected function wrapping its own auth). One function in, seven kinds of judgment out. The host never learns what a derivation is; LHC never learns what a credential is.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/epic.md:25:This epic makes LHC's derivations real. A host hands `createSdk` one model-call function and a per-kind model assignment config; LHC's adapter renders each derivation's prompt, routes the call through the host's function to the assigned provider and model, shapes the output into the derived form, and classifies failures into Epic 02's retry machinery. An opt-in verification suite proves the whole path against a real endpoint, ending in the real-inference leg of Epic 04's lifecycle exercise — the integration-readiness gate the deterministic leg explicitly deferred.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/epic.md:31:1. [Inference Wiring and Model Assignment](#flow-1-inference-wiring-and-model-assignment) — the injected function and per-kind config, validated loudly at construction. AC 1.1–1.4\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/epic.md:44:- The model-call injection seam: `createSdk` accepts one host-supplied function plus per-kind model assignments, as an alternative to direct provider injection (which stays, for tests)\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/epic.md:73:A host constructs the SDK one of two ways. The existing way stands unchanged: inject a `DerivationProvider` directly (the deterministic test provider, or any custom implementation). The new way is the production path: inject an `inference` config — one model-call function plus a model assignment for each of the seven derivation kinds. The SDK builds the real adapter (Flow 2) over them. Exactly one of the two must be present; both or neither is a construction error.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/epic.md:75:The model-call function is the only thing that ever touches credentials, and it lives entirely on the host's side of the boundary. Its contract is deliberately minimal: take a provider name, a model id, and a messages array; return text or a structured failure. The provider and model strings are opaque routing keys to LHC — they mean whatever the host's function makes them mean. A host authed to four lanes routes per-call on the provider string; mixed assignments across lanes in one config are the normal case, not a special one.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/epic.md:77:The model assignment config is flat and complete: every one of the seven kinds names its provider, model, and prompt. No defaults that silently route — a missing kind, an unknown kind, or an unknown prompt name fails construction loudly, naming the problem. Configuring the seven assignments is a conscious setup step by design: a silent wrong default is exactly the hard-to-see failure mode this validation exists to prevent.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/epic.md:81:- **AC-1.1**: `createSdk` accepts exactly one of `provider` (a `DerivationProvider`, unchanged) or `inference` (model-call function + assignments). Both supplied, or neither, is a caller error naming the rule.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/epic.md:84:- **AC-1.4**: Per-call routing: each drained work item looks up its kind's assignment and the function receives that assignment's provider and model strings. Different kinds routing to different providers in one config work item-by-item with no cross-kind interference.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/epic.md:93:The adapter is the inference epic's core deliverable: an implementation of Epic 02's `DerivationProvider` whose seven operations each render an LHC-owned prompt around the operation's input, call the injected function with the kind's assignment, and shape the response into form content. The seam does not move — handlers keep calling `provider.smoothPrompt(...)` exactly as they do against the deterministic provider; nothing in `domains/` knows whether marker strings or a real model sits behind the interface.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/epic.md:99:Every form a real model produces also records its provenance: which provider, model, and prompt version made it. That stamp is what makes the dial-in period's evals attributable — output quality questions become joinable to the assignment that produced the output.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/epic.md:111:- **TC-2.1** (AC-2.1, AC-2.2, AC-2.5): With a scripted fake function returning distinct canned text per kind: drain a seeded thread → all seven form kinds land `ready` with content from the canned responses; outcomes/receipts match the record (not the canned text); each form's metadata carries the assignment that produced it.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/epic.md:142:- **AC-4.1**: The real-inference suite is opt-in on key presence and its outcome is always visible: ran (with the model assignments used) or not-ran (with reason) in the suite output. Absence of the key can never produce a silent pass. The suite's model-call function lives in test code, implements the AC-1.2 contract, and reaches a real endpoint.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/epic.md:171:- **TC-5.3** (AC-5.5): Epic 03's regression legs under the new trigger: flipped results stay flipped across subsequent turn closes; compact resets the boundary to the compact point with a fresh full tail; same record + same budgets → same boundary trajectory; injected advance failure → intake unaffected, boundary unchanged, status shows the over-budget zone, next turn close heals.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/epic.md:213:**The model assignment config** — flat, complete, three fields per kind. Worked example with mixed lanes (the normal case):\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/epic.md:218:  assignments: {\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/epic.md:300:| AC-5.5 | TC-5.3 |\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/coverage.md:45:| AC-5.3 | Story 6 | TC-5.3 | Covered |\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/coverage.md:75:| Startup validates all seven assignments before first derivation use | Story 6 | TC-5.1 | Covered |\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/coverage.md:77:| Validation failures leave capture running and failures queryable | Story 6 | TC-5.3 | Covered |\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/coverage.md:92:| Story 6: Startup Validation and Assignment Config | semantic rule + metadata / additive | Bad lanes are visible before first use, and capture survives them. | Config, validation, reporting, and failure behavior | Reporting must work without assuming TUI; validation failure must not block capture | Keep together because all behavior governs assignment readiness before derivation use |\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/tech-design.md:12:The design splits inference into two layers with different owners. The **semantic layer** is LHC's and lives in a new `src/inference/` area: seven prompt templates, output shaping, per-kind model assignment, failure classification, provenance stamping. The **transport layer** is the host's and crosses the boundary as exactly one function: `(provider, model, messages) → text or structured failure`. The host's function is the only code that knows what `\"anthropic\"` means; LHC treats provider and model strings as opaque routing keys carried from config to call. This is the seam decision recorded in `post-epic-4-work.md`, and it is why the PI extension's eventual contribution is ~15 lines while the seven prompts stay tunable inside LHC without touching any host.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/tech-design.md:23:- **DD-4: Provenance rides the `ProviderResult` and is copied, never authored.** The ok branch gains `provenance?: { provider, model, prompt }`. The adapter stamps it (it alone knows the assignment); handlers copy it into `DerivedFormMetadata.provenance` alongside the existing mechanical stamps. The deterministic provider simply never sets it. This keeps the \"metadata is mechanically stamped\" invariant: provenance comes from config-known strings, not model output.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/tech-design.md:24:- **DD-5: Construction is `provider` XOR `inference`.** `createSdk` accepts exactly one. The `inference` path validates the assignment map (all seven `FormKind`s present, every prompt name known) and builds the adapter; the `provider` path is unchanged and keeps the deterministic provider as the test default. Both/neither is a `TypeError` at construction, matching the existing config-mistakes-throw contract.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/tech-design.md:66:├── sdk.ts                              # MODIFIED: provider XOR inference; assignment validation;\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/tech-design.md:92:| `sdk.ts` | MODIFIED | XOR validation, assignment validation (all seven kinds, known prompts), adapter construction; SDK-only export surface | `inference/*`, existing | AC-1.1, AC-1.3, AC-6.1 |\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/tech-design.md:107:Construction is where every mistake should die. `createSdk` gains the XOR check first (before any other validation, so the error names the rule, not a downstream symptom), then — on the `inference` path — assignment validation: iterate the seven `FormKind`s, require each present with non-empty `provider`/`model` strings and a `prompt` name found in `PROMPT_REGISTRY`; reject unknown kind keys. The resolved config then feeds `createInferenceProvider`, whose return value drops into the existing `resolved.provider` slot — everything downstream of construction is Epic 02 code.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/tech-design.md:116:    Note over Host,SDK: AC-1.1, AC-1.3: XOR + assignment validation\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/tech-design.md:117:    Host->>SDK: createSdk({ inference: { call, assignments } })\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/tech-design.md:123:    ADP->>Host: call({ provider, model, messages }) — the kind's assignment\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/tech-design.md:128:The routing fact worth stating precisely: the adapter closes over the assignment map at construction; each operation looks up its own kind's entry at call time. There is no per-thread or per-item routing state — two drained items of different kinds calling different lanes is just two closure lookups.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/tech-design.md:132:Each adapter operation is the same five-step pipeline: (1) bound the input where DD-7 applies, (2) `render` the kind's prompt template into a messages array, (3) `safeCall` the host function with the kind's assignment, (4) reject empty/whitespace text as `empty_output`, (5) return `{ ok: true, text, provenance }`. The operations differ only in which template renders and which input fields embed — the pipeline is one private function parameterized by kind.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/tech-design.md:246:  assignments: Record<FormKind, ModelAssignment>; // all seven, validated complete\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/tech-design.md:298:Construction errors are `TypeError`s naming the violated rule (existing contract): `\"createSdk config: exactly one of provider or inference\"`, `\"inference.assignments missing kind chunk_summary_brief\"`, `\"inference.assignments.smoothed_prompt.prompt names unknown template 'smoothing-v2'\"`, `\"view.visibility.maxTokens must be > targetTokens\"`. Operating failures never throw: they are `ProviderResult` failures with `reason` = the failure kind, flowing into form state via the unchanged Epic 02 paths (`failed` with reason `provider_failure` at exhaustion, terminal kinds immediately).\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/tech-design.md:326:| 2: Seam + assignments | XOR, validation, adapter construction wiring | 1.1–1.4 | Validation drift from `FormKind` (mitigation: iterate the exported union, never a literal list) |\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/test-plan.md:19:- **Terminal dispositions** (DD-1, reported-then-deleted): TC-1.1 asserts the drain report's `disposition='done'` entries **and that the work rows are gone** (raw read: zero rows for the drained ids); TC-1.8's exhaustion leg asserts the report's `failed_terminal` plus the form `failed` carrying reason + final attempts, row deleted; TC-5.4 (mutations suite) asserts the stale item reports `stale_discarded`, row deleted, rebuilt form untouched; the supersede path is asserted in TC-5.3 on the **MutationResult** (`superseded` ids listed; raw read confirms rows deleted — a drain never sees them); TC-4.6 asserts the blocked-source item reports `failed_terminal` with the form `blocked`, row deleted.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/test-plan.md:85:| TC-5.3 | All forms ready; edit | Immediately post-return: dependent forms `pending`, queue holds replacement items at new source version; replacement item ids include that source version; superseded queued ids on the MutationResult, rows deleted |\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:250:- **TC-5.3** (AC-5.3): Same key sent to two threads → both record.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:502:| AC-5.3 | TC-5.3 | 2 |\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/stories/02-inference-seam-and-model-assignment.md:6:Add the `createSdk` inference construction path: one host model-call function plus complete per-kind model assignments.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/stories/02-inference-seam-and-model-assignment.md:13:**Objective:** Establish the production inference seam and validate model assignments loudly at SDK construction.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/stories/02-inference-seam-and-model-assignment.md:15:**Scope In:** `provider` XOR `inference`, `ModelCall` contract, complete assignment validation, adapter construction wiring, and per-call provider/model routing.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/stories/02-inference-seam-and-model-assignment.md:24:- **AC-1.1**: `createSdk` accepts exactly one of `provider` (a `DerivationProvider`, unchanged) or `inference` (model-call function + assignments). Both supplied, or neither, is a caller error naming the rule.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/stories/02-inference-seam-and-model-assignment.md:27:- **AC-1.4**: Per-call routing: each drained work item looks up its kind's assignment and the function receives that assignment's provider and model strings. Different kinds routing to different providers in one config work item-by-item with no cross-kind interference.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/stories/02-inference-seam-and-model-assignment.md:34:  - unknown kind key in assignments returns `TypeError` naming it\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/stories/02-inference-seam-and-model-assignment.md:37:  - empty `provider` or `model` string in one assignment returns `TypeError`\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/stories/02-inference-seam-and-model-assignment.md:51:This story establishes the SDK construction boundary for real inference. `createSdk` accepts exactly one provider arrival path: direct `provider` injection or `inference` config. On the inference path, SDK validation resolves the assignment map and builds the real adapter into the same `DerivationProvider` slot the deterministic provider already uses.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/stories/02-inference-seam-and-model-assignment.md:53:The external boundary is the host-supplied `ModelCall`. LHC owns provider/model assignment and prompt selection; the host owns credentialed transport.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/stories/02-inference-seam-and-model-assignment.md:133:  assignments: Record<FormKind, ModelAssignment>;\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/stories/02-inference-seam-and-model-assignment.md:149:  assignments: {\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/stories/02-inference-seam-and-model-assignment.md:169:| empty provider/model | assignment field and kind |\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/stories/02-inference-seam-and-model-assignment.md:174:- Do not accept partial assignment maps.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/stories/02-inference-seam-and-model-assignment.md:180:- Entrypoint: `createSdk({ inference: { call, assignments } })`.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/stories/02-inference-seam-and-model-assignment.md:198:- Inference assignments validate all seven `FormKind`s, reject unknown keys, reject unknown prompts, and reject empty provider/model strings.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/04-fork-as-new-thread.md:72:| Thread creation | `packages/pi-lhc/src/lifecycle/thread-resolution.ts`, LHC `threads` operations |\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:6:Load seven derivation assignments from config, validate lanes at session start, report unreachable lanes, and keep capture running through validation failures.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:13:**Objective:** Bad derivation lanes are visible before first use, assignment overrides take effect on next session start, and capture survives validation failures.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:18:- Shipped default assignments.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:21:- Interactive and headless reporting for unreachable lanes.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:27:- Status-bar/footer UX beyond actionable startup-validation reporting.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:35:**AC-5.1:** At session start the extension validates all seven model assignments against PI's registry before first derivation use.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:37:**TC-5.1** — All seven assignments validate against the registry at session start before first use.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:39:**AC-5.2:** An unreachable lane — not logged in, unknown model, unknown provider — is reported before first use, naming the derivation kind, the (provider, model), and the corrective action. The report reaches the user in interactive and headless modes alike (guarded on UI availability, never assuming a TUI).\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:41:**TC-5.2** — An unreachable lane reports kind + (provider, model) + fix; the report appears in a headless mode, not only the TUI.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:45:**TC-5.3** — A validation failure leaves capture running; the affected lane's derivations fail classified and queryable.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:47:**AC-5.4:** Model assignments load from the extension's config. Each of the seven derivation kinds resolves to a (provider, model, prompt), where the prompt names a registered prompt. The epic ships with default assignments so derivations function; assignment quality is a dial-in concern, not a build gate.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:51:**AC-5.5:** A user override — a different provider, model, or prompt for any kind — takes effect on the next session start with no code change. An incomplete or unknown assignment fails loudly at initialization with an actionable error, never a silent skip or a placeholder default that masks the misconfiguration.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:55:**TC-5.6** — An incomplete or unknown assignment fails loudly at initialization with an actionable error; no placeholder default masks it.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:62:Story 6 owns assignment loading and startup validation before first derivation use. Assignments provide provider, model, and prompt for all seven derivation kinds, with user overrides applied on the next start.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:64:Validation has two layers: assignment shape is fail-loud at initialization, while reachability distinguishes unknown provider/model from configured-auth absence. Capture continues even when validation reports unreachable lanes.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:72:- Red should pin all seven assignments, override behavior, loud shape failures, headless reporting, and capture continuity.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:82:| Assignment loading | `packages/pi-lhc/src/inference/assignments.ts` |\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:83:| Startup validation | `packages/pi-lhc/src/inference/startup-validation.ts` |\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:86:| Tests | `packages/pi-lhc/test/inference/startup-validation.test.ts`, `packages/pi-lhc/test/inference/assignments.test.ts` |\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:102:| TC-5.1 | `test/inference/startup-validation.test.ts` | All seven assignments validate at start before first derivation use. |\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:103:| TC-5.2 | `test/inference/startup-validation.test.ts` | Unreachable lane report names derivation kind, provider/model, reason, and fix; report appears in headless mode and state health. |\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:104:| TC-5.3 | `test/inference/startup-validation.test.ts` | Validation failure leaves capture running; affected derivations fail classified and queryable. |\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:105:| TC-5.4 | `test/inference/assignments.test.ts` | Shipped defaults load provider/model/prompt for all seven kinds and prompts resolve. |\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:106:| TC-5.5 | `test/inference/assignments.test.ts` | User override of provider/model/prompt takes effect on next start. |\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:107:| TC-5.6 | `test/inference/assignments.test.ts` | Missing kind, unknown prompt, incomplete assignment, or placeholder fails loudly at initialization. |\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:121:- Do not silently substitute default assignments over incomplete or unknown user config.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:128:- Registration/default path: assignments load from extension config, shape validation runs, reachability probe reports through UI/headless channel and `SessionState.health`.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:133:- Targeted: `pnpm --filter pi-lhc verify -- test/inference/startup-validation.test.ts test/inference/assignments.test.ts`\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:147:- Missing, unknown, incomplete, or placeholder assignments fail loudly.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/02-derivation-pipeline/epic.md:244:- **TC-5.3** (AC-5.3): Edit while forms are `ready` → immediately after return, all dependent forms `pending`, queue holds their work.\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/stories/05-real-inference-suite-and-capstone.md:24:- **AC-4.1**: The real-inference suite is opt-in on key presence and its outcome is always visible: ran (with the model assignments used) or not-ran (with reason) in the suite output. Absence of the key can never produce a silent pass. The suite's model-call function lives in test code, implements the AC-1.2 contract, and reaches a real endpoint.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/01-session-lifecycle-and-thread-resolution.md:91:| Thread resolution | `packages/pi-lhc/src/lifecycle/thread-resolution.ts` |\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/01-session-lifecycle-and-thread-resolution.md:95:| Tests | `packages/pi-lhc/test/lifecycle/instance.test.ts`, `packages/pi-lhc/test/lifecycle/thread-resolution.test.ts` |\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/01-session-lifecycle-and-thread-resolution.md:113:| TC-1.2 | `test/lifecycle/thread-resolution.test.ts` | Existing launch resolves through registry and creates no second thread. |\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/01-session-lifecycle-and-thread-resolution.md:114:| TC-1.3 | `test/lifecycle/thread-resolution.test.ts` | Fresh PI context objects across hooks do not break capture; retained holder contains no prior context object. |\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/01-session-lifecycle-and-thread-resolution.md:116:| TC-1.5 | `test/lifecycle/thread-resolution.test.ts` | Reload discards in-memory holder, resolves the same thread id from registry, and continues on that thread. |\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/01-session-lifecycle-and-thread-resolution.md:117:| TC-1.6 | `test/lifecycle/thread-resolution.test.ts` | No flag, full id, partial id, continue, and bad id resolve exactly as specified; bad id creates no thread. |\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/01-session-lifecycle-and-thread-resolution.md:118:| TC-1.7 | `test/lifecycle/thread-resolution.test.ts` | Resume picker lists only current-cwd threads with title and creation time; empty cwd returns an empty selection state. |\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/01-session-lifecycle-and-thread-resolution.md:124:| Persistence / Restart | `test/lifecycle/thread-resolution.test.ts` | Discard the in-memory holder, re-resolve the same thread by id from the registry, then capture continues on it. | The AC says reload reconstructs; this proves it survives process loss without retained PI objects. |\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/01-session-lifecycle-and-thread-resolution.md:140:- Registration/default path: launch flags route to `thread-resolution.ts` / `picker.ts`; resolved thread initializes `instance.ts` in background mode.\n./docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/01-session-lifecycle-and-thread-resolution.md:145:- Targeted: `pnpm --filter pi-lhc verify -- test/lifecycle/instance.test.ts test/lifecycle/thread-resolution.test.ts`\n./docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/05-derivation-inference/artifacts/02-inference-seam-and-model-assignment/004-story-orchestrate-resume.json:1:{\"command\":\"story-orchestrate resume\",\"version\":1,\"status\":\"needs-user-decision\",\"outcome\":\"needs-ruling\",\"result\